entries above the main diagonal are zeros:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true, since the result of $A+{A}^{T}$ has all the entries above the main diagonal equal to zero: \n\n$$\n{A}^{T}+A=[\\begin{aligned}16 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "017114c3-a1a2-5d5e-aec5-f7291e21000d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323985} {:answer/id #uuid "23edc6a5-133c-5266-b6f2-96c0c94a31f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324267} {:answer/id #uuid "65328dfd-ddc6-512e-aa6e-ec778103ab31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324807} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ce84b6-e829-50fb-8784-7ae9dd928eec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325810} {:answer/id #uuid "f204210a-2a56-5ba1-bb31-fca5ab8c400d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3ce84b6-e829-50fb-8784-7ae9dd928eec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325810} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2d9cf43-baa4-5293-a4f3-835ff4ab6fd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326655}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7fc84187-05b2-57ce-8e48-261250d11c80" :question/math-academy-id "q-42116" :question/problem "Consider the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is lower triangular\n- ${A}^{T}-A$ is lower triangular\n- $A$ is upper triangular" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, since all the entries above the main diagonal are zeros:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, since the result of ${A}^{T}-A$ has non-zero entries above the main diagonal:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}-A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false, since $A$ has non-zero entries below the main diagonal\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335970 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b04cce7-f7e1-514a-bf46-6bdc2f41e305" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333397} {:answer/id #uuid "3a4005dd-77d3-5d4b-a72c-2cf5db39a286" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333531} {:answer/id #uuid "52569706-e3d9-5a3d-bc15-02a4fd34fdec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333748} {:answer/id #uuid "60d691ce-29b6-5220-898b-1b6a3afe6708" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333869} {:answer/id #uuid "613a72a0-7650-5c25-bf03-340b66e2443b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "613a72a0-7650-5c25-bf03-340b66e2443b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333875} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3deb942-3ce8-54f2-a03d-3da4ced6794e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335569}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "deb97145-eaf3-5dcc-a36b-24fae2c52b5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-68754" :question/problem "Consider the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -7 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 8 \\\\ 7 & -8 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is upper triangular\n- $A$ is lower triangular\n- $A+{A}^{T}$ is upper triangular" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false, since $A$ has non-zero entries below the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -7 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 8 \\\\ 7 & -8 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, since $A$ has non-zero entries above the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -7 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 8 \\\\ 7 & -8 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true, since the result of $A+{A}^{T}$ has all the entries below the main diagonal equal to zero:\n\n$$\nA+{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342194 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dbca51b-7f54-5a7a-ba5f-3d88cb4bc95a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339529} {:answer/id #uuid "55b791d8-4371-5bf9-bd1e-a54e720de779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339913} {:answer/id #uuid "7b095170-c16f-5974-ab98-b3690a252ff3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340228} {:answer/id #uuid "caef5ea6-785c-5dcb-bc6b-8144853d5c55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340925} {:answer/id #uuid "e368e4ef-99eb-518e-bdb0-6a3a39964aef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e368e4ef-99eb-518e-bdb0-6a3a39964aef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341141} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0391ab3-a597-51aa-a3d8-ce337f570b0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341744}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e68d4b2f-c3b6-584c-8430-662a571e8316" :question/math-academy-id "q-74318" :question/problem "Consider the matrix \n$C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $C$ is upper triangular\n- $C$ is lower triangular\n- ${C}^{T}$ is upper triangular" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. All the entries below the main diagonal of $C$ are zeros:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, since $C$ has non-zero entries above the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false, since ${C}^{T}$ has non-zero entries below the main diagonal:\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342195 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34777553-5006-5f5c-9727-020effdb1863" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339594} {:answer/id #uuid "3dc969e4-1f40-5c0a-b425-d47fcc951c13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339680} {:answer/id #uuid "961268ff-d9a5-5b91-83a8-57bc579b7229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340482} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ed66f7-ff3c-5b64-aded-bf5edcca033c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340854} {:answer/id #uuid "f144d22f-284c-5b5a-bb1b-1dd2f910f9dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f144d22f-284c-5b5a-bb1b-1dd2f910f9dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341254} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5048421-85b3-572d-8328-77b24b9d125e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341698}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10915988-162b-5dec-9de2-5241d5fc3202" :question/math-academy-id "q-74319" :question/problem "Consider the matrix \n$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $D$ is upper triangular\n\nII. $D$ is lower triangular\n\nIII. ${D}^{T}$ is upper triangular" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false, since $D$ has non-zero entries below the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. All the entries above the main diagonal of $D$ are zeros:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. All the entries below the main diagonal of ${D}^{T}$ are zeros:\n\n$$\n{D}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Correct Statements About a Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119860 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119675 :question/id #uuid "118ad24e-627f-51ff-9b9c-9319c50956f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6303" :question/problem "![](images/85/8573aedcd26cba038b2db072560cd3d6ba7676542888a2d9b8a356713eaf6724.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf r (t) = \\langle \\sin t ,\\: \\cos t, \\: t \\rangle$\nfrom  $t =0$ to $t = 2\\pi.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\sqrt{2}\\,t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1889ba1a-09d4-5021-9ed2-3e531f8d5ef0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80732c44-5634-545c-8ff4-349bbfb066cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e} + e^{2} - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117784} {:answer/id #uuid "8580dd5e-760c-5889-98d9-960bbf1db32d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{2} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117785} {:answer/id #uuid "a62f4b65-d49a-56e3-9420-a6fd0017782c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{2} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117786} {:answer/id #uuid "0730b85e-9b1c-5d8e-bd2e-6057303c06e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e^{2}} + e^{2} - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117787} {:answer/id #uuid "1dd0a809-82f3-5432-8d1d-1a4d827a4d3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117788}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ee6767b3-a496-5ed5-83f0-c6e7926f26ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117783}] :question/id #uuid "788d951c-3d3b-5459-ac8b-6aca1943a14a" :question/math-academy-id "q-33759" :question/problem "![](images/c7/c76cff47179efaaf490430856b8db1f2b85f0d2000d34725a9c40a9e150ccf6b.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $r(t) = ⟨\\ln t, 2t, t^{2}⟩$\nfrom $t = 1$ to $t = e$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e49651a-bdf3-5daa-a8ac-b0f9a1acf337" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309453} {:answer/id #uuid "683d57e8-3476-5871-b8ce-02f1e4add2e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309550} {:answer/id #uuid "8f378059-5350-57fd-9b5f-1f9ef63dc60c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309892} {:answer/id #uuid "b4bd39a7-2f29-5a63-9c46-e2b53b0a1e5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310211} {:answer/id #uuid "d51c476d-8e1e-5c39-9e95-2b6de637ee4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4bd39a7-2f29-5a63-9c46-e2b53b0a1e5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310211} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3ec7fac-5ac8-5f09-95e8-e286bea551a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311408}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d7f25b66-94f4-5ecd-88ef-6b78d26ced87" :question/math-academy-id "q-33779" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $f(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{t}^{2}⟩$\nfrom $t=1$ to $t=3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${f}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{f}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\sqrt{3}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{3}t⟩ \\\\ ‖{f}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3}t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)+3{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $t>0$ for $t\\in [1,3].$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}‖{f}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =9-1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262810 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12089a6a-a880-58fd-8015-e683d64044cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260033} {:answer/id #uuid "2e79b9bf-496a-5b86-95db-3519ffcc1fd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260295} {:answer/id #uuid "32f21b1e-e0d3-5e8b-a4f6-207a056c3331" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260335} {:answer/id #uuid "bcdb4104-831e-55b0-8287-359801a260f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261542} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb575b0-eaae-5420-9b87-61fc65e0c4d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcb575b0-eaae-5420-9b87-61fc65e0c4d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262084} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce714977-afa6-5cfe-b61e-62d5fd5e4fd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262485}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6796746e-0500-559b-91cc-a530cd306e54" :question/math-academy-id "q-127635" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,\\,\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3},\\,{t}^{2}⟩$ from $t=-1$ to $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,{t}^{2},\\,2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}+{({t}^{2})}^{2}+{(2t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4{t}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+2)^{2}} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({t}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+2t){|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{0}^{3}}{3}+2(0))-(\\frac{(-1)^{3}}{3}+2(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345259 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "107d5a2a-4f6f-52c4-a7ea-a3c986f47360" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342381} {:answer/id #uuid "12208fda-53a7-5d69-9f6a-dbdffb153e06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342399} {:answer/id #uuid "768320a8-bc68-56f3-9b17-f4dad32ac2f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343266} {:answer/id #uuid "9381484a-6981-59d3-b123-6f51531c898a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343530} {:answer/id #uuid "bd7ef7a7-f907-538f-b138-4c1ddb333884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "107d5a2a-4f6f-52c4-a7ea-a3c986f47360" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342381} :answer-field/id #uuid "20642a04-5ca1-5221-ae4c-b1d1be5781d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344550}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "06fe26ef-4ec2-5c23-a26d-637e986a7e20" :question/math-academy-id "q-76906" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,4,\\,{t}^{2}⟩$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,0,\\,2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+0+{(2t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\,t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $t\\ge 0$ for $t\\in [0,2].$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2\\sqrt{2}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}({t}^{2}){|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345260 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63bfa53b-e47b-50cc-89fd-5a4e4528fce4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343084} {:answer/id #uuid "79ea6bc3-b230-56b5-bb39-3960352c7365" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343288} {:answer/id #uuid "87e6f26e-9f09-5844-8065-ef122df54dbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343414} {:answer/id #uuid "9e0991c3-27a4-50a9-8460-379ec4e4da59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343628} {:answer/id #uuid "d6cbc97b-38b5-54a3-92bc-84bf1dbbd503" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344123}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6cbc97b-38b5-54a3-92bc-84bf1dbbd503" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344123} :answer-field/id #uuid "60342b90-65d0-553f-8346-360744bd9458" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344653}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ccf4d567-d105-5c9b-9e19-604b98b9eea2" :question/math-academy-id "q-76908" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3}⟩$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2t,\\,2{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(2t)}^{2}+{(2{t}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}+4{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(1+2{t}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =1+2{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(1+2{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(t+\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3})|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =3+18-0 \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345261 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f74f6bc-5e7b-58d7-b7fc-c365ef4e92f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342896} {:answer/id #uuid "57499e39-7bdc-59ad-a99d-360f1fc7b2ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342966} {:answer/id #uuid "6677667e-3442-5a82-af63-c15f1638bd7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343106} {:answer/id #uuid "fd321b38-e06d-5b93-9ae1-a114b299ba87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344462} {:answer/id #uuid "fdf30511-d971-58e6-a9d7-f63d74b0483c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6677667e-3442-5a82-af63-c15f1638bd7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343106} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7fb0837-5c09-5cad-8bec-dfcdb3722f64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344897}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f089db80-ac84-508f-a172-79a9d0b01dd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-76914" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2},\\,2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,3t,\\,4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}^{2}+(3t)^{2}+{(4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{t}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡2t+9{t}^{2}+16{t}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡2t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{t}^{2}+9{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =5t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $t\\ge 0$ for $t\\in [0,2].$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}5t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{5{t}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345270 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b5a67bb-0704-5374-80b7-f3c08e8d80a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342601} {:answer/id #uuid "46234d75-8481-586d-a8d6-e8817f41182e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342815} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba3f5f9-1429-57ca-8bd7-30d8b83aa72f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343454} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b57ee9-5770-5bad-bc47-e53eff6914ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344087} {:answer/id #uuid "e992c30b-4dd9-5634-88df-cea97771fa79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46234d75-8481-586d-a8d6-e8817f41182e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342815} :answer-field/id #uuid "05a88dee-a59b-585a-bbc1-cb4004578b85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344504}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "50a8400d-aac2-5c82-9900-5410f81b5445" :question/math-academy-id "q-77064" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨10,\\,\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,{t}^{2}-1,\\,2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{({t}^{2}-1)}^{2}+{(2t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}+1+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+t)|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =9+3-0 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371721 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0c3c144-342c-568c-8008-dfc3bbf3e55b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371723} {:answer/id #uuid "819bb05a-265c-5492-a6f2-63e69e3366e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371724} {:answer/id #uuid "7cc99bc0-2cfc-57b8-ad76-ee0deba98fe7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371725} {:answer/id #uuid "9773cc34-e79f-5a4a-b9ef-d818a89d1a1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371726} {:answer/id #uuid "e1a2a18e-b24b-5257-8c86-2b702af0f038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0c3c144-342c-568c-8008-dfc3bbf3e55b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371723} :answer-field/id #uuid "af468363-83b9-5ccc-8120-0215969486e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371722}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0760b1bf-45a5-5aed-9a7d-860971967403" :question/math-academy-id "q-127633" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(1)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}t\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a712676f-2c97-5cb7-966d-09797a5f8021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373225} {:answer/id #uuid "cc3eecdc-de51-5ae0-8de8-01a1ae198ae8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373226} {:answer/id #uuid "21bd52ca-4869-53d6-8d7a-03019261b5ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373227} {:answer/id #uuid "70e3eb1f-dcfb-53cc-99f6-b1609cba2012" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373228} {:answer/id #uuid "f5d56a9c-d543-5f82-9abd-1d18c7ec7d92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5d56a9c-d543-5f82-9abd-1d18c7ec7d92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373229} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f0cc1ce-5d3d-51df-bd70-280476103aae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373224}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c6a58127-1393-5bb0-acf9-ab77e9d7ffad" :question/math-academy-id "q-76909" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨2-{t}^{2},\\,{t}^{2}+4,\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}⟩$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2t,\\,2t,\\,t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-2t)}^{2}+{(2t)}^{2}+{(t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =3t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $t\\ge 0$ for $t\\in [0,4].$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}3t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}({t}^{2}){|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ffcfd43-b15b-55e0-9453-963134a6d995" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "130" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373232} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9391e4-7424-54d0-a36c-f5b21aa3b4e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373233} {:answer/id #uuid "726ddffe-91b8-5f33-bfb8-b9930883abab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373234} {:answer/id #uuid "4048f826-7e00-5c3f-ad4e-3b51a78539bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373235} {:answer/id #uuid "4ab3aac1-31a4-5103-a8fc-ee5e47c2090b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ffcfd43-b15b-55e0-9453-963134a6d995" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "130" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373232} :answer-field/id #uuid "f96bcd49-a02f-5bd7-ab21-7a05bacf2ad7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373231}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e0a56e4-79a9-58b5-babc-ce3937cee4f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-76910" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨\\frac{4-\\sqrt{3}{t}^{2}}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,{t}^{3},\\,t+5⟩$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4-\\sqrt{3}{t}^{2}}{\\sqrt{2}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+5)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\sqrt{6}t,\\,3{t}^{2},\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\sqrt{6}t)}^{2}+{(3{t}^{2})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6{t}^{2}+9{t}^{4}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1+3{t}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =1+3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}(1+3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t+{t}^{3}){|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =5+125-0 \\\\  & =130.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Arc Length of a Three-Dimensional Spatial Curve"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124437 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124241 :question/id #uuid "225b0bca-3520-58ab-bd36-0e3de337b604" :question/math-academy-id "e-3068" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P(2,1,8)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_y = \\dfrac{4x}y - 4x^2y,$ find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ So, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(2,1,8)$ will be given by the partial derivative $f_y(2,1).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $f_y(x,y)$ at $(2,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,1) & =\\frac{4(2)}{(1)}-4(2)^{2}(1) \\\\  & =8-16 \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "049e8586-8348-50be-ac56-4ea79eba43cd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048707 :topic/math-academy-id 1929 :topic/title "Introduction to Partial Derivatives"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124957 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bf0df7e-022b-5418-9924-365a7162eff3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321611} {:answer/id #uuid "3fa3c59a-6624-5598-a1a5-f291f19779f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321648} {:answer/id #uuid "44d2ebf6-d34d-5c73-86a9-e87ea0a289de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321692} {:answer/id #uuid "9a6fc4ed-289d-56da-b773-3e27dbdc2b79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322372} {:answer/id #uuid "bd68ef18-667b-55d2-a107-cbd29381e44b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44d2ebf6-d34d-5c73-86a9-e87ea0a289de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321692} :answer-field/id #uuid "090efca4-d7f7-51f0-927e-9f82cbd619ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323185}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63eca657-711b-57df-bd85-bce2f11b802d" :question/math-academy-id "q-36216" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=3.$ Given that the point $P(2,3,9)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=6x-y$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(2,3,9)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(2,3).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(2,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,3) & =6(2)-(3)=9\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124987 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddb9cb29-68e3-5973-a4a4-8242c4c3bfdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122523} {:answer/id #uuid "2d162824-da2b-546d-9abb-654bfdacfdb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122524} {:answer/id #uuid "520b12a4-9c6f-59fa-92da-2250edd1ff5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122525} {:answer/id #uuid "59570931-e9e1-5343-a190-74e25d21e6c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122526} {:answer/id #uuid "76e3e537-863d-50f7-922d-42e905ecbbc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122527}] :answer-field/id #uuid "bf52da3a-42bd-52d4-baf6-4f9b1486ac5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122522}] :question/id #uuid "76fa0a1f-c88e-5c19-9675-405dad1d4057" :question/math-academy-id "q-36463" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z = f(x, y)$. The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y = 0$. Given that the point $P$ with $x$-coordinate $\\frac{π}{2}$ lies on $C$, and that $f_{x} = y\\cos x + \\cos y$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268881 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45a98813-2da9-54b1-be36-e284f308a481" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266767} {:answer/id #uuid "51b0aabb-8305-5c29-9b5f-9de63ccafeb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266874} {:answer/id #uuid "727b509d-e810-5caf-ac73-b9babe4fc8e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267148} {:answer/id #uuid "9da497a9-5263-520b-9a2e-92a6d26e637b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267563} {:answer/id #uuid "a70b4400-cd37-5893-85fa-f7497e058575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45a98813-2da9-54b1-be36-e284f308a481" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266767} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccf81380-c294-5e58-9e12-11b8701e5c19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268774}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ea3a7950-5517-53eb-8054-3498346f46a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-132579" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P$ with $x$-coordinate $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+xy$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-1-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268882 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "226ad3f6-7e7b-5847-9314-07159469d3e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266452} {:answer/id #uuid "34c500a5-2bfc-5d7d-af3c-b5668516b746" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266606} {:answer/id #uuid "97cf4b27-8cb2-5296-96fe-683228875813" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267503} {:answer/id #uuid "a5bb424a-1a21-5451-bf5c-bf751e9a4479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267641} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ddca65-ba60-5a83-84d5-38e7eeab917a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "226ad3f6-7e7b-5847-9314-07159469d3e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266452} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9bb7d99-021d-5d65-952d-0bbc4117f169" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268768}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "09f5edfe-2ef7-5aa3-b9a3-3d13ae59dd49" :question/math-academy-id "q-132584" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=-3.$ Given that the point $P(1,-3,8)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}+y\\sqrt{x}$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=-3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(1,-3,8)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,-3).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,-3) & =\\frac{{1}^{2}+1}{{1}^{2}(-3)^{2}}+(-3)\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}-3(1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{25}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335768 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c9a56e0-5972-5209-9d5c-ea16e82e995f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333100} {:answer/id #uuid "66686a01-21cc-56d7-951a-bacb93258c0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333921} {:answer/id #uuid "79013707-fe29-522d-ac8d-ac18b46d1880" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334075} {:answer/id #uuid "989e0c9d-3b24-5671-9e42-b86c26764caf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334342} {:answer/id #uuid "b3886518-c45d-5987-8dfe-39a52ed04936" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66686a01-21cc-56d7-951a-bacb93258c0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333921} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a4e3654-a019-5e30-92b9-ed3bae770d8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335446}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f6c71da-f5b0-5c03-871d-e07fb324730c" :question/math-academy-id "q-64968" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P$ with $y$-coordinate $0$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\sqrt{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+\\frac{\\pi }{4})$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $y=0$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =\\sqrt{2}(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338912 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34d5f7cf-613e-525d-93c2-0c7080720d91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336530} {:answer/id #uuid "6b07d985-3564-5d0a-b070-9e5e048515e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336980} {:answer/id #uuid "c6298338-e8f0-5052-8bae-ef3ddf73ce7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337789} {:answer/id #uuid "e6ad9eb0-f197-5640-819b-488c340e4e28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338046} {:answer/id #uuid "f52f84db-f262-57d1-9c5e-5a630266362b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6ad9eb0-f197-5640-819b-488c340e4e28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338046} :answer-field/id #uuid "7598b8df-f496-507a-9973-2189f2324d0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338478}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6efe9457-81ed-5961-99c6-f2de702a7e78" :question/math-academy-id "q-71198" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P$ with $y$-coordinate $1$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=3{x}^{2}y-6x{y}^{2}$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $y=1$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(2,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(2,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,1) & =3(2)^{2}(1)-6(2)(1)^{2} \\\\  & =12-12 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383293 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4997e6e-db2a-59f0-8089-3716fd8ae7ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383295} {:answer/id #uuid "e6b94256-12a4-5e4a-a7be-2faa95e83174" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383296} {:answer/id #uuid "aa5682d4-84d4-5541-90d8-7e7040c8235e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383297} {:answer/id #uuid "1592e766-be1b-5bdf-ac2c-4264db8109b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383298} {:answer/id #uuid "749b8f4c-62f7-5c59-bc47-3128b8a6da53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa5682d4-84d4-5541-90d8-7e7040c8235e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383297} :answer-field/id #uuid "24582cee-9319-59e6-9a06-ddc0dc01268c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383294}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b9055ebf-a856-5760-9126-377331881bc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-132578" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P(1,2,5)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=4{x}^{2}y$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(1,2,5)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,2).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,2) & =4(1)^{2}(2)=8\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383300 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd5e40ce-803c-5d96-872d-3f0e492eadbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383302} {:answer/id #uuid "720aed43-145e-53b9-bdfc-ad1cab577b46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383303} {:answer/id #uuid "fb3769df-f295-584f-8d17-4cc6daf9c592" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383304} {:answer/id #uuid "4b3cb4a3-a5c9-5109-9c27-f48151dd1ac0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383305} {:answer/id #uuid "42983750-aaf0-5078-93dc-54997c0e3fa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb3769df-f295-584f-8d17-4cc6daf9c592" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383304} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e22321c-1630-581b-a2cf-b182baccc9d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383301}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a3109e9-f31c-5d6f-a6b9-341111eed328" :question/math-academy-id "q-132580" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=e.$ Given that the point $P(e,2,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{{e}^{y}}{x}$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=e.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(e,2,3)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(e,2).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(e,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(e,2) & =2(2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{e}=4-e\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383307 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e7ce596-2d30-587f-8420-45e62e13d5dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383309} {:answer/id #uuid "877b2fa4-194f-5fc4-8176-fe7bfbedcc56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\pi }{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383310} {:answer/id #uuid "8ca68eba-afe1-5148-a902-7b75f1cf86fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383311} {:answer/id #uuid "625a56ce-727a-5b05-a3f7-9bccf25f5e8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383312} {:answer/id #uuid "2f162d22-7bab-5326-997a-02d3e40fadea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "877b2fa4-194f-5fc4-8176-fe7bfbedcc56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\pi }{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383310} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c8364fd-cb71-5621-b790-1b21e9be9448" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383308}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8c4b5aa7-27d5-5875-960f-3c04a61ab9fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-132582" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=\\pi .$ Given that the point $P$ with $x$-coordinate $1$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=(x+y){e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)}$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=\\pi .$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $x=1$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,\\pi ).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,\\pi ) & =(1+\\pi ){e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)} \\\\  & =(1+\\pi ){e}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\pi }{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385487 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6357e5c2-cf46-5660-bc20-49dbba19cc28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385489} {:answer/id #uuid "c55a41e3-729e-57f0-b1aa-89cceb118ec8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385490} {:answer/id #uuid "7b13627d-a272-58c9-8614-2ccdaa4b3963" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385491} {:answer/id #uuid "a48f0d5e-1c10-5086-b78b-c0af0a0d5cb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385492} {:answer/id #uuid "e97a3c40-2850-570e-ac0a-9580b1fbaf26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e97a3c40-2850-570e-ac0a-9580b1fbaf26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385493} :answer-field/id #uuid "81978c17-aece-5d30-9a3d-6fc52afbe445" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385488}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b3d97ced-0267-5370-864a-580ea21d98cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-71199" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P$ with $x$-coordinate $3$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}}}{y-x}$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $x=3$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(3,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(3,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(3,2) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}-2(2)^{2}}}{2-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{9-8}}{-1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425118 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09b9a14b-262a-515e-bd82-1f704a821349" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425090} {:answer/id #uuid "2590094e-7f95-52df-bdd8-abb0af831110" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425096} {:answer/id #uuid "336e9f14-d6c6-5f83-897b-4c14eb89e5be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425097} {:answer/id #uuid "91000328-2b30-5049-9390-2fee6404bd0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425109} {:answer/id #uuid "e4a190e1-968a-5e68-8061-84d550c81b2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09b9a14b-262a-515e-bd82-1f704a821349" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425090} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d50699f-3ed7-5626-8b63-c6ce285403be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425116}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5cb4c36d-f79b-5525-8cf7-91f3e27f40c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-64966" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={2}^{x+y}y(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1)$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(0,1,0)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(0,1).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1) & ={2}^{0+1}(1)(0\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124438 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124242 :question/id #uuid "70bcf628-cf1f-5da0-85b7-19d0f514ccd8" :question/math-academy-id "e-3069" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=\\pi.$ Given that the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_x = y\\cos(xy) + \\cos y,$ find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z = f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=\\pi.$ So, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\dfrac {\\partial z} {\\partial x} \\Bigg|_{(1,\\pi)}.$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\dfrac {\\partial z} {\\partial x}$ at $(1,\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\pi )} & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot \\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =\\pi (-1)+(-1) \\\\  & =-\\pi -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,\\pi )} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\pi )}(x-1) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(-\\pi -1)(x-1) \\\\ z+1 & =(-\\pi -1)x+\\pi +1 \\\\ z & =-(\\pi +1)x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above represents a line in the $xz$-plane. The $xz$-plane through the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ has the equation $y=\\pi.$\n\nSo, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ is given by the intersection of the following planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\pi  \\\\ z=-(\\pi +1)x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5685f7-71b2-58ae-8c61-f555b355d92f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050635 :topic/math-academy-id 418 :topic/title "Equations of Lines in Point-Slope Form"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124958 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0315c355-94e8-5b85-bc0d-c26e707e74cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=\\frac{4}{5}y-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321176} {:answer/id #uuid "9db2c19a-f9cc-5cd6-8a22-46dc603af896" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322389} {:answer/id #uuid "bc186f0e-14b9-56f8-a702-fc4364be3a61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322605} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c279f3-89f6-5f4b-b92c-736849d41a97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=-\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322911} {:answer/id #uuid "f880ea2c-47c4-55a4-afa7-5ab8c96b72f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=\\frac{5}{2}y-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323101}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9db2c19a-f9cc-5cd6-8a22-46dc603af896" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322389} :answer-field/id #uuid "36f4a259-af06-543c-939e-9833986d33ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323278}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "491a90b9-f33c-5937-8491-8e8447d7cf82" :question/math-academy-id "q-36219" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,2,4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\frac{3x+y}{\\sqrt{6xy+{y}^{2}}}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,2,4)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(1,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,2) & =\\frac{3(1)+(2)}{\\sqrt{6(1)(2)+(2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(1,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-4 & =\\frac{5}{4}(y-2) \\\\ z & =\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,2,4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124976 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1f69732-5e50-5b65-89ef-95823d50c6f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y = 1,; z = 4x - 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122457} {:answer/id #uuid "d8d284c3-b75c-5802-a424-0e940b3ffd75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y = 1; z = 3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122458} {:answer/id #uuid "32364905-605a-5bf2-913a-acf3e415ffa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y = 1; z = 2x - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122459} {:answer/id #uuid "782ea77e-69d2-5c59-b771-1bad28e70e30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y = 1; z = 2 - 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122460} {:answer/id #uuid "7fcf1b4e-725d-5976-aa0e-41725cf3a02a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y = 1; z = 3x - 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122461}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c9c1faf1-2d7b-5e78-a408-90a8cb93ac6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122456} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d90446d-468e-5505-8c08-1f46b0193618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=2-2x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385352} {:answer/id #uuid "e6f459ee-dee1-5d31-a4d4-71a204434fda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1, \\\\ z=4x-4\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385353} {:answer/id #uuid "7621cca1-35e1-500f-b716-2d3e51c1d38e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=3x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385354} {:answer/id #uuid "c43b040e-4e33-5989-9fb5-766a3deff3c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=3x-3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385355} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f78f87-4d49-51d8-924b-bd1e64d65e49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=2x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c43b040e-4e33-5989-9fb5-766a3deff3c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=3x-3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385355} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec17033d-7e32-53b3-b00f-f4c5e370a468" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385351}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "faf15e19-a012-50bb-842f-791131393cd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-36369" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,1,0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=3x+6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,1,0)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,1)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,1)} & =3(1)+6(1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =3+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,1)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,1)}(x-1) \\\\ z-0 & =3(x-1) \\\\ z & =3x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,1,0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=3x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268883 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "106c62f5-6a15-5356-9819-bac29ddd8e00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=-2 \\\\ z=-4x+9\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266282} {:answer/id #uuid "4cdcc8ed-799e-5868-8d9c-e5cddb211a2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=-2 \\\\ z=-4x+21\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266824} {:answer/id #uuid "502e00e2-2746-5fc9-8240-98511ddcf672" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=-2 \\\\ z=2x+9\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266856} {:answer/id #uuid "50dcaed5-8a14-56a6-9c8d-e4ff85b79e32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=-2 \\\\ z=-2x+15\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266866} {:answer/id #uuid "9b80ced7-aa68-5bf4-8dc4-6402f10538d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=-2 \\\\ z=2x+21\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cdcc8ed-799e-5868-8d9c-e5cddb211a2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=-2 \\\\ z=-4x+21\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266824} :answer-field/id #uuid "49d228b2-8aea-57a5-b2ca-483e7c7b3e29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268529}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "484f8b36-97b4-5e4f-a875-d05f61c48bf6" :question/math-academy-id "q-132585" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=-2.$ Given that the point $P(4,-2,5)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=y\\sqrt{x}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(4,-2,5)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(4,-2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(4,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(4,-2)} & =(-2)\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =-2(2) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(4,-2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(4,-2)}(x-4) \\\\ z-5 & =-4(x-4) \\\\ z & =-4x+21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(4,-2,5)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-2 \\\\ z=-4x+21\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268884 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0839792e-52f8-55c0-96a0-793207bc2a23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=6x-5\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266218} {:answer/id #uuid "1f23aed8-c0d3-5c83-bb76-cf7ec415c01d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=3x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266413} {:answer/id #uuid "af853526-8839-56ef-bbab-ba5bdb85abf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=6x+3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267724} {:answer/id #uuid "d3488bb1-cb08-55c3-baf7-71b2ba5e7513" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=3x-5\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268028} {:answer/id #uuid "e94121d6-a767-5df0-af24-04d3cf8908a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=4x+3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0839792e-52f8-55c0-96a0-793207bc2a23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=6x-5\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266218} :answer-field/id #uuid "edc73d7a-8739-5705-bbef-4b94db05673c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268821}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c434702-618c-5a71-8a4a-aadc6bd7fee6" :question/math-academy-id "q-132587" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P(1,2,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=2xy+2$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,2,1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,2)} & =2(1)(2)+2 \\\\  & =4+2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,2)}(x-1) \\\\ z-1 & =6(x-1) \\\\ z & =6x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,2,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=6x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268885 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3535f187-54bf-56fb-8443-7dab32a9c79d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=2y-3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425099} {:answer/id #uuid "3c2c97aa-3243-5467-ac12-075c53d9654e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y+3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425100} {:answer/id #uuid "43046f4c-8850-5dea-9f71-c5e3ebec2500" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=2y-1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425101} {:answer/id #uuid "5b8e160b-491c-5c8e-996d-6c8ececaab38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425102} {:answer/id #uuid "77cfa3de-e27b-5fe1-b628-9f80c5d48c58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77cfa3de-e27b-5fe1-b628-9f80c5d48c58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425106} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ab8e018-d349-5c74-b2e8-1930a7b5eded" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268476}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c5dd738-1c94-548e-aa53-3b5d32dc4abc" :question/math-academy-id "q-132589" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=e.$ Given that the point $P(e,2,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=e.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(e,2,3)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(e,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(e,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(e,2) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(e,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(e,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-3 & =1(y-2) \\\\ z & =y+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(e,2,3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y+1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268886 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b49c0a3-842a-5e4f-8c9f-bcdacd6482db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=x+2\\pi \\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266525} {:answer/id #uuid "3fa233f4-64d9-5e7c-9bf4-5d6de3431a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=2x-2\\pi \\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266718} {:answer/id #uuid "5722d450-4de6-5584-a7a4-4c5a28d785ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=-2x+\\pi \\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266918} {:answer/id #uuid "8af7c44c-aec2-5a26-b0b4-846d06ad93e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=x-\\pi \\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267389} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f58a5e-b636-567f-a4e0-4e5efca66f70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=-2x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5722d450-4de6-5584-a7a4-4c5a28d785ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=-2x+\\pi \\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266918} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a7709cb-f264-5087-8141-9df67bddfa9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268475}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "90718d28-70a4-5587-a36e-fa57ec8d26d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-132590" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2,2\\pi )$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{xy}{\\pi }$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2,2\\pi )$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-\\pi /2,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-\\pi /2,2)} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})(2)}{\\pi } \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(-\\pi /2,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-\\pi /2,2)}(x-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\ z-2\\pi  & =-2(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ z & =-2x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2,2\\pi )$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=-2x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268887 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c12ac46-65be-560d-9f11-fe9d7449b413" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=4y-\\pi \\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266818} {:answer/id #uuid "5d615371-d30a-5061-a8d8-53c4cddd2cf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=2y+\\pi \\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266968} {:answer/id #uuid "69b370cf-2c77-55a6-8fef-0dc70f82194d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=2y-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267080} {:answer/id #uuid "e9d90385-1c8d-507f-a431-c96e74e25c9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=4y-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268226} {:answer/id #uuid "f9acbcf2-7dff-58f1-a11d-910c512a2b90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=4y\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c12ac46-65be-560d-9f11-fe9d7449b413" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=4y-\\pi \\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266818} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4e7d076-c660-5037-a4ff-af8a315c239c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268835}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22a41e65-3d40-52de-a0eb-a4c7353c0c15" :question/math-academy-id "q-132593" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(2,\\pi /4)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =4\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(2,\\pi /4)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(2,\\pi /4)}(y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ z-0 & =4(y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ z & =4y-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=4y-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268888 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "050297e0-14ea-5f6a-aba2-48bd43bf28c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{3}{2}y+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266190} {:answer/id #uuid "22f28163-088c-5d95-9a9a-2af096df3146" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=\\frac{3}{2}y-1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266462} {:answer/id #uuid "33de049e-5b2f-5cc1-9723-2daf22363b92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{1}{4}y-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266600} {:answer/id #uuid "7a114e7f-482b-5446-b9af-8ac3b7d1f2ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=\\frac{1}{2}y+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267221} {:answer/id #uuid "d93410da-a216-5a9b-90e2-fe7a6af727ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d93410da-a216-5a9b-90e2-fe7a6af727ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268070} :answer-field/id #uuid "92a71cca-98d2-5245-a4d1-fd31406052e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268663}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac300975-45c9-5a2c-a8b5-5d2addf48a83" :question/math-academy-id "q-132595" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=\\sqrt{2}.$ Given that the point $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=-\\frac{2{x}^{2}+y}{{y}^{3}}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=\\sqrt{2}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(\\sqrt{2},2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(\\sqrt{2},2) & =-\\frac{2(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+2}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4+2}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-\\frac{1}{2} & =-\\frac{3}{4}(y-2) \\\\ z & =-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323792 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "204035e4-46d4-534d-ba05-15ce0e052f85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=4y+8\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321387} {:answer/id #uuid "5b70aad1-ccca-5779-affa-e5700fd0cc6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=-4x\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321868} {:answer/id #uuid "7510c1fa-11fc-530d-ac02-4cbbe0845097" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=-4y+8\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322059} {:answer/id #uuid "85c6028a-d0f8-58ef-9cb9-0c6994f1659c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=4x+8\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322206} {:answer/id #uuid "9cadbd18-60fa-56f2-aef5-823c9dd90a52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=4y-8\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7510c1fa-11fc-530d-ac02-4cbbe0845097" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=-4y+8\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322059} :answer-field/id #uuid "57b4da25-e31a-52b9-9b49-54d616020d35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323339}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "daecb367-4754-591f-93db-eaa083b20ffe" :question/math-academy-id "q-36301" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,0,8)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+4y+x$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(-2,0,8)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,0)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(-2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,0)} & =(-2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+4(0)+(-2) \\\\  & =-2-2 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(-2,0)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,0)}(y-0) \\\\ z-8 & =-4(y-0) \\\\ z & =-4y+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,0,8)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=-4y+8\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323802 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03a88646-da7b-5cc5-a6ca-e3694eb78578" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=4x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321182} {:answer/id #uuid "31748a7a-7455-5dc9-aebc-92650c97c472" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=4x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321526} {:answer/id #uuid "a14281d6-156f-5f48-beed-65acb59f3aec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=4y-12\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322421} {:answer/id #uuid "af9c34b4-7ca6-552a-a577-8a9187d131ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=-4y+12\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322525} {:answer/id #uuid "d0f4b2b7-738a-52b8-9ea5-229baa45ac6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=4y-4\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0f4b2b7-738a-52b8-9ea5-229baa45ac6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=4y-4\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322780} :answer-field/id #uuid "41b9822b-8388-5077-b4c1-9ebc7d355fb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323295}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "12d0b28e-9c11-5054-bcb2-feb86bd6fc4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-36441" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=0.5.$ Given that the point $P(0.5,2,4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=y+2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+\\frac{1}{x}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=0.5.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(0.5,2,4)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(0.5,2)}.$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(0.5,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(0.5,2)} & =2+2(2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡((0.5)(2))+\\frac{1}{0.5} \\\\  & =2+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(0.5,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(0.5,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-4 & =4(y-2) \\\\ z & =4y-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0.5,2,4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=4y-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c5ba22e-dbcf-5fa2-95ae-a8ddaa66f720" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=6y-3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425107} {:answer/id #uuid "8020f82d-21fe-5f0d-b435-1b1601809556" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=4y-5\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425108} {:answer/id #uuid "9da8c96e-1efe-59f9-a9dc-682a12d5da97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=2y-3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425110} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d9df8e-4012-5e27-bf02-9250e930e1d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=4y-3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425111} {:answer/id #uuid "f37ea42d-7c61-5d99-8c24-718ac347ab47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=2y-5\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425114}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9da8c96e-1efe-59f9-a9dc-682a12d5da97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=2y-3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425110} :answer-field/id #uuid "335b522d-b8a5-5ac7-8b9d-2985076f5c17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338374}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3868b943-72a6-5a73-9952-d94521bce0ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-71209" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,2,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}={x}^{2}y+2x-y$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(-2,2,1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(-2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-2,2) & =(-2)^{2}(2)+2(-2)-(2) \\\\  & =8-4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(-2,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-1 & =2(y-2) \\\\ z & =2y-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,2,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=2y-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338917 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "156f0e60-a655-5cee-ace2-00f94feeb9b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336234} {:answer/id #uuid "3998953b-84b0-5889-81f7-4dc1224db93d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336573} {:answer/id #uuid "4603346d-02b6-5575-9939-64549e4f33fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336681} {:answer/id #uuid "6b358259-0538-5cb3-840e-3b6005519fca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=-x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336981} {:answer/id #uuid "ef59035a-1a91-53fe-8a72-a5d6bc5d3dc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3998953b-84b0-5889-81f7-4dc1224db93d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=x+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336573} :answer-field/id #uuid "67c2ebb0-7f04-5390-9b3f-6c5b9ac02ee1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338458}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ceda561f-8269-5197-b05a-efc41af9a9a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-71218" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,2)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(0,1,2)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(0,1)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(0,1)} & =(0)^{2}+(1)^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1) \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(0,1)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(0,1)}(x-0) \\\\ z-2 & =1(x) \\\\ z & =x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,2)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391977 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "803d5b94-2569-5c7b-acab-8a683fa5915c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=5x-14\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391979} {:answer/id #uuid "8a8e72bd-76d3-5c76-a6f8-4ccd3cada7f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=5x-6\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391980} {:answer/id #uuid "ab96cbaa-b455-527d-b0f1-8f47476751ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=5y+4\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391981} {:answer/id #uuid "838d45e1-ad2e-54ae-9309-e08be1a015e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=5x+6\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391982} {:answer/id #uuid "92928948-a2b1-52a4-8197-0abe60da91ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=-5x-6\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a8e72bd-76d3-5c76-a6f8-4ccd3cada7f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=5x-6\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391980} :answer-field/id #uuid "2712fe89-2404-5ec9-a1bd-af26bb9d16b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391978}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "376329be-a379-553e-b714-516f831f127f" :question/math-academy-id "q-36372" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=0.$ Given that the point $P(2,0,4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={e}^{y}+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=0.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(2,0,4)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(2,0)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,0) & ={e}^{(0)}+2(2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =1+4 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(2,0)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(2,0)}(x-2) \\\\ z-4 & =5(x-2) \\\\ z & =5x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,0,4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=5x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407316 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f49be2f-9fbd-57d9-8743-c7f792e14929" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-y+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407305} {:answer/id #uuid "40205211-9c82-5bbc-b29c-5211d1a45bbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-2y-1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407306} {:answer/id #uuid "645649e7-f6ca-5dcf-a129-028646be2cfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=y-1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407308} {:answer/id #uuid "8d3f6706-e60e-5d15-b360-58385706ee4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-2y+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407309} {:answer/id #uuid "d4018ef0-2dba-5e3f-8527-3ea960bf4de4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-y-1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4018ef0-2dba-5e3f-8527-3ea960bf4de4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-y-1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407311} :answer-field/id #uuid "e94262ee-f4dd-5ac5-a015-9c5cf4798aa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407315}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "05b78370-0b37-5800-aa28-6b6b17964b90" :question/math-academy-id "q-71216" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,0,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=x{e}^{xy}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,0,-1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(1,0)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1\\cdot 0}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,0)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(1,0)}(y-0) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(-1)y \\\\ z & =-y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,0,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}]}
